ould be affected by changes in equipment efficiency requirements."}],"plainSummary":"This joint resolution would block a Department of Energy rule that set stricter energy efficiency standards for walk-in coolers and freezers used in commercial settings. The rule, finalized in December 2024, would have required walk-in refrigeration equipment to meet new energy consumption limits starting in 2027 and 2028. If passed, the resolution would nullify those standards and leave the existing, less stringent requirements in place.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys food.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"M001233","score":0.01466652534196303},"overlapCount":11},"meaning":{"percentile":55.29263290739864,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6843407726501993}},{"billId":"119-hr-29","bioguideId":"J000301","cycle":2026,"score":0.00798706357571671,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":10000,"donorShare":0.009165482791806059,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.0044518059274486575},{"industry":"government","donorAmount":7500,"donorShare":0.006874112093854544,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.0035352576482680516}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.6843407726501993},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-29","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"29","title":"Laken Riley Act","introducedDate":"2025-01-03","latestActionDate":"2025-02-10","latestActionText":"Read the second time. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 10.","policyArea":"Immigration","subjects":["Border security and unlawful immigration","Civil actions and liability","Crimes against property","Criminal investigation, prosecution, interrogation","Detention of persons","Immigration status and procedures","Intergovernmental relations","Refugees, asylum, displaced persons","State and local government operations","Visas and passports"],"sponsorBioguideId":"C001129","cosponsorCount":53,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/29","industries":[{"industry":"government","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly imposes new detention mandates on DHS and creates new litigation exposure for federal agencies, affecting federal and state government operations and budgets."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill creates new grounds for state litigation against the federal government and expands detention requirements, increasing demand for legal services in immigration and intergovernmental litigation."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would require the Department of Homeland Security to detain individuals who are in the country without authorization and have been arrested for or charged with burglary, theft, larceny, or shoplifting. It would also allow state governments to sue the federal government for injunctive relief when federal immigration enforcement decisions or failures—such as releasing individuals from custody or not fulfilling visa or asylum processing requirements—cause harm to the state or its residents, including financial harm exceeding $100.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People applying to live here, and their families here. People applying to come here, and their families here.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"S000250","score":0.02595346470272257},"overlapCount":50},"meaning":{"percentile":38.507651049061366,"median":0.01073720309570498,"n":6339,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.6843407726501993}}],"votes":[{"id":"a64a90b759595d68f3a8d502","billId":"119-hr-7008","date":"2026-07-22T16:51:00-04:00","question":"Yea-and-Nay","result":"Passed","position":"Y